/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  THE BASIS-RECONCILIATION WITNESS (spike S2, ratified 2026-08-03 --
  docs/design/basis-reconciliation-spike.md).

  The sour-water system: ammonia and carbon dioxide in water.  The package is
  MINIMAL BUT NOT COINCIDENTAL -- each of the five roles the spike names is
  filled by exactly one component, so no check below can pass by an accident
  of symmetry:

      water     the solvent
      NH3       the CATION route: its declared bridge is
                `aqueousMapping ( { species NH4; nu 1; } { species H; nu -1; } )`
                -- one component, one cation row in the block
      CO2       the ANION route (HCO3-, CO3--) and, at the same time, the
                AQUEOUS MOLECULAR species in equilibrium (CO2aq)
      ethanol   a neutral molecular VOLATILE, nonionising and CONDENSABLE --
                it sits on the Raoult backbone and gets NO species row
      N2        nonionising and NONCONDENSABLE -- a permanent gas, so it
                takes the HENRY rung through its own gas-liquid record and
                DOES get a species row: the dissolved N2

  THE TWO NONIONISING COMPONENTS BEHAVE DIFFERENTLY, and that is the point
  of having both.  `aqueousSpeciation none;` says a component does not join
  the ionic NETWORK -- it does not say the component is absent from the
  aqueous phase.  Ethanol is condensable and priced on the backbone, so the
  block has nothing to say about it; N2 is a permanent gas dissolved by
  Henry, so the block carries a NEUTRAL `N2` row that must collapse to
  itself and contribute exactly zero to Sum z*n.  One file shows both, which
  is cheaper than a paragraph explaining that "nonionising" is a statement
  about charge and not about presence.

  Why not fewer.  A single symmetric salt closes the charge balance by
  symmetry and a sign error would be invisible; here the cation (NH4+) and
  the anion (HCO3-) come from DIFFERENT components, in different amounts, so
  Sum z*n is a real test.  Two networks are active at once (`ammonia` and
  `carbonate`), which is also what makes the pH a solved quantity rather than
  a constant.

  WHY EVERY COMPONENT IS MOLECULAR.  The chain hands this material to a unit
  running a plain molecular world (see system/flowsheetDict).  A dissolved
  SALT could not cross that boundary at all -- a nonvolatile salt has no
  route through the ideal-gas reference, and the engine refuses rather than
  invent one.  Choosing NH3/CO2 for the ionic roles keeps the boundary
  crossable while still putting a genuine cation and a genuine anion in the
  block: the chemistry is the ions', the components stay molecular.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

recordType    thermophysicalPropertySystem;
schemaVersion 2;

components    ( NH3  CO2  water  ethanol  N2 );

equilibrium
{
    formulation electrolyteGammaPhi;

    aqueous
    {
        activityModel { model davies; }
    }

    vapour    { fugacityModel idealGas; }

    volatiles ( NH3  CO2  water  ethanol  N2 );
}

//  The molecular backbone is ONE component wide here (ethanol), so a curated
//  pair table would price nothing the answer depends on.  The ideal is
//  therefore DECLARED and DELIMITED rather than assumed -- authorisation is
//  this block's presence, and it names the component it covers.  The spike
//  is about the BASIS crossing a model boundary, not about the accuracy of
//  the backbone; saying so here is cheaper than a gamma nobody reads.
approximations
{
    idealMolecularVLE
    {
        components ( ethanol  N2 );          // delimited -- never global
        reason "the basis-carriage witness: the molecular backbone is one
                component wide, so an ideal gamma is the honest declaration
                rather than a fitted pair nothing here would exercise";
    }
}
